#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179462 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18af80e5-cd0f-5a1d-b299-2956fd886c5d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Particular Solution of a Logistic Growth Initial Value Problem to Make Predictions"}] :question/id #uuid "77bb1db2-e2fb-5126-a116-fce07c309d0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7407" :question/problem "The growth rate of the hare population $P(t)$ in a particular forest is jointly proportional to the hare population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the forest's carrying capacity and the hare population. The constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,01$ and the carrying capacity is $10\\,000.$ How many hares will there be in the forest after $7$ years given that there were $2\\,500$ hares initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "Since the hare population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the hare population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the hare population, the hare population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{a}{1+Ce^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality $k=0.000\\,01,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=10\\,000.$ Therefore, the hare population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 0.000\\,01 P(10\\,000-P) .\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) \n= \\dfrac{10\\,000}{1+Ce^{-(10\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,01)t}}= \\dfrac{10\\,000}{1+Ce^{-0.1t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=2\\,500$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =2\\,500 \\\\ \\frac{10\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.1(0)}} & =2\\,500 \\\\ \\frac{10\\,000}{1+C} & =2\\,500 \\\\ \\frac{1+C}{10\\,000} & =\\frac{1}{2\\,500} \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{10\\,000}{2\\,500} \\\\ 1+C & =4 \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\frac{10\\,000}{1+3 e^{-0.1 t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of hares in the forest after $7$ years, we substitute $t=7$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(7) & =\\frac{10\\,000}{1+3{e}^{-0.1\\cdot 7}} \\\\  & ≈4\\,016.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179463 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e71ba74-f952-56ef-885c-a32c4a4aec20" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Logistic Growth Initial Value Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "e53b71f8-4ea8-50e7-81c6-62fbd2ac54ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-7408" :question/problem "The rate of growth of the panda population $P(t)$ in a particular forest follows the logistic growth model is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 0.001\\,25 P(800-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the beginning of the observations. Find an expression for the panda population after $t$ years, given that there were $400$ pandas initially." :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) = \\dfrac{a}{1+Ce^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.001\\,25,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=800.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\nP(t) \n= \\dfrac{800}{1+Ce^{-(800\\cdot 0.001\\,25)t}}= \\dfrac{800}{1+Ce^{-t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=400$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-0}} & =400 \\\\ \\frac{800}{1+C} & =400 \\\\ \\frac{1+C}{800} & =\\frac{1}{400} \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{800}{400} \\\\ C & =2-1 \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the panda population is given by\n\n$$\nP(t)= \\dfrac{800}{1+e^{-t}} .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e71ba74-f952-56ef-885c-a32c4a4aec20" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Logistic Growth Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8912babc-6e4b-5f3e-912d-6a0065743b20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{25}{1+{e}^{-0.009t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327347} {:answer/id #uuid "1e992a9d-587d-5289-ae53-0e7d820fc4e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{5}{1+25{e}^{-1.17t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327348} {:answer/id #uuid "391985b4-fcd1-50ad-890f-1b7e26744141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{25}{1+{e}^{-t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327349} {:answer/id #uuid "e453d900-273f-5a1e-851e-0d6d5a484e2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{130}{1+25{e}^{-0.009t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327350} {:answer/id #uuid "797ca0e7-6bec-5dab-9b05-d0a0c20f2ad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{130}{1+25{e}^{-1.17t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "797ca0e7-6bec-5dab-9b05-d0a0c20f2ad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{130}{1+25{e}^{-1.17t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327351} :answer-field/id #uuid "de450828-f588-5f1a-88f1-da16f9577e9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ca1449f-8949-5519-8f98-ed37dccf1a4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-119861" :question/problem "The rate of growth in the number of fishes $P(t)$ with a fungal infection in a certain pond is jointly proportional to the number of fishes that have been infected at time $t$ and the number that have not been infected. If the constant of proportionality is $0.009$ and the carrying capacity is $130,$ then find an expression for the number of fishes with fungal infection, given that there were $5$ fishes infected initially." :question/worked-solution "Since the growth rate of infected fishes is *jointly* proportional to the number of fishes that have been infected at time $t,$ and the number that have not, the number of infected fishes follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.009,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=130.$ Therefore, the growth of the number of fishes with fungal infection is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.009P(130-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{130}{1+C{e}^{-(130\\cdot 0.009)t}}=\\frac{130}{1+C{e}^{-1.17t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=5$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{130}{1+C{e}^{-1.17\\cdot 0}} & =5 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{130}{5} \\\\ C & =26-1 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t)=\\frac{130}{1+25{e}^{-1.17t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18af80e5-cd0f-5a1d-b299-2956fd886c5d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Particular Solution of a Logistic Growth Initial Value Problem to Make Predictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc5a3bd2-32fd-50bc-b18d-9843dbda43a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327354} {:answer/id #uuid "8f6b953d-3ac6-54e5-a1c4-5579ce19b0ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\,296" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327355} {:answer/id #uuid "54a2609e-7724-5d06-8c34-e5b59cc61ae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,638" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327356} {:answer/id #uuid "6ecd2d6f-e151-5d0b-ba28-6a81c1980514" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327357} {:answer/id #uuid "18236403-e1d5-5d60-adba-83c15e215827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f6b953d-3ac6-54e5-a1c4-5579ce19b0ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\,296" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327355} :answer-field/id #uuid "49671687-4a8b-5d56-906f-715ca3c0205c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "01e090ea-18cf-5d07-9563-7039d4d1d497" :question/math-academy-id "q-120253" :question/problem "The growth rate in the number $P(t)$ of files infected with a virus in a computer is jointly proportional to the number of infected files at time $t,$ measured in hours, and the difference between the computer's carrying capacity and the number of infected files. The constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,005$ and the carrying capacity is $100\\,000.$ How many files will have the virus after $12$ hours given that there were $1000$ files with the virus initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "Since the number of files infected with the virus' growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the number of infected files at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the number of infected files, the number of files infected with the virus follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,005,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=100\\,000.$ Therefore, the growth of the number of files infected with the virus is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,005P(100\\,000-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{100\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(100\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,005)t}}=\\frac{100\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.5t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=1000$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =1000 \\\\ \\frac{100\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.5(0)}} & =1000 \\\\ 1+C & =100 \\\\ C & =99.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{100\\,000}{1+99{e}^{-0.5\\cdot t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=12$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(12) & =\\frac{100\\,000}{1+99{e}^{-0.5\\cdot 12}} \\\\  & ≈80\\,296.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327359 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18af80e5-cd0f-5a1d-b299-2956fd886c5d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Particular Solution of a Logistic Growth Initial Value Problem to Make Predictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0022dcba-6cad-574b-b71e-4fa8ab87afaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "548" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0022dcba-6cad-574b-b71e-4fa8ab87afaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "548" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327361} :answer-field/id #uuid "746cee96-5e9f-5474-9c16-10527f2d168e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ebc94644-35a7-5ccd-b4dd-ea6583f0712d" :question/math-academy-id "q-347875" :question/problem "The population $P(t)$ of rabbits on a particular island follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P-0.0002{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. If there were $400$ rabbits on the island initially, how many rabbits inhabited the island after $3$ years? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAfter $3$ years, approximately\n{{field-1}} rabbits inhabited the island." :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.2P-0.0002{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.0002P(1000-P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.0002,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=1000.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1000}{1+C{e}^{-(1000\\cdot 0.0002)t}}=\\frac{1000}{1+C{e}^{-0.2t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=400$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1000}{1+C{e}^{-0.2(0)}} & =400 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{1000}{400} \\\\ C & =2.5-1 \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{1000}{1+1.5{e}^{-0.2t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=3$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =\\frac{1000}{1+1.5{e}^{-0.2(3)}} \\\\  & ≈548.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $3$ years, approximately $548$ rabbits inhabited the island."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327362 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e71ba74-f952-56ef-885c-a32c4a4aec20" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Logistic Growth Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d807166-1d92-5d35-8b97-350a0171ca76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7e^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327364} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b391eb-74f1-59d0-9560-7d57c357dc98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7e^{-0.005t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d807166-1d92-5d35-8b97-350a0171ca76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7e^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327364} :answer-field/id #uuid "10162ae4-12f0-5d53-a516-997edbc6bdb9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3bdf0d0-1ee1-5303-bb41-ac3247396350" :question/math-academy-id "q-353253" :question/problem "The growth rate of the number of residents using a new fitness app, $P(t),$ in a small town is jointly proportional to the number of residents using the app at time $t,$ measured in months, and the difference between the town’s maximum potential users and the number of residents currently using the app.\n\nGiven that the constant of proportionality is $0.005$ and the maximum potential number of users is $800,$ find an expression for the number of residents using the app after $t$ months, given that there were $100$ users initially.\n\n| $P(t)\\,=\\,\\,$ | $800$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1\\,+\\,$ {{field-1}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Since the number of residents using the app grows at a rate that is *jointly* proportional to the number of residents using the app at time $t$ and the difference between the maximum potential number of users and the number currently using the app, the number of residents using the app follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.005,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=800.$ Therefore, the growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.005\\,P(800-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-(800\\cdot 0.005)t}}=\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-4t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=100$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{800}{1+C{e}^{-4\\cdot 0}} & =100 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{800}{100} \\\\ C & =8-1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{800}{1+7{e}^{-4t}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18af80e5-cd0f-5a1d-b299-2956fd886c5d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Particular Solution of a Logistic Growth Initial Value Problem to Make Predictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8691b2a6-c748-5021-b507-44c96ea3713d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4872" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179220} {:answer/id #uuid "88ba5a31-6b61-5f52-8f33-d6de20464f6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4354" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179221} {:answer/id #uuid "aff57b8d-f271-5427-bd4a-35a20ddcdaad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4582" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179222} {:answer/id #uuid "b1583f25-d5d8-51b5-b04f-8efe2af554ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4972" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179223} {:answer/id #uuid "c370ed19-9e73-5f99-a4ed-a9f34eb6d2a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4342" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c370ed19-9e73-5f99-a4ed-a9f34eb6d2a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4342" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179224} :answer-field/id #uuid "3efd3de1-b598-582c-b19a-3c6dd81576b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "94524d73-2bf5-594f-b12c-32a707870543" :question/math-academy-id "q-83771" :question/problem "The growth rate of the squirrel population $P(t)$ in a certain forest is jointly proportional to the squirrel population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the forest's carrying capacity and the squirrel population. The constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,01$ and the carrying capacity is $5000.$ How many squirrels will there be in the forest after $10$ years given that there were $4000$ squirrels initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Since the squirrel population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the squirrel population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the squirrel population, the squirrel population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,01,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=5000.$ Therefore, the squirrel population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,01P(5000-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{5000}{1+C{e}^{-(5000\\cdot 0.000\\,01)t}}=\\frac{5000}{1+C{e}^{-0.05t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=4000$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =4000 \\\\ \\frac{5000}{1+C{e}^{-0.05(0)}} & =4000 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ C & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{5000}{1+0.25{e}^{-0.05\\cdot t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=10$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =\\frac{5000}{1+0.25{e}^{-0.05\\cdot 10}} \\\\  & ≈4342.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e71ba74-f952-56ef-885c-a32c4a4aec20" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Logistic Growth Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a23ab289-7408-5f72-b763-93b399d59fd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{10\\,000}{1+4{e}^{-2t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327368} {:answer/id #uuid "d9dc8695-230d-5236-b5b3-c467a3463d25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{10\\,000}{1+4{e}^{-0.1t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327369} {:answer/id #uuid "701d448d-ecf2-59e0-b307-8df5d49a1e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{10\\,000}{1+5{e}^{-0.2t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327370} {:answer/id #uuid "0488013f-329e-55ab-abc0-d8cf0acfcb5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{10\\,000}{1+9{e}^{-0.2t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327371} {:answer/id #uuid "963cd396-bb73-5c75-ba38-1371f63c5389" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{10\\,000}{1+5{e}^{-2t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0488013f-329e-55ab-abc0-d8cf0acfcb5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{10\\,000}{1+9{e}^{-0.2t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327371} :answer-field/id #uuid "413a0b70-8446-552e-bb92-a85faa7bb468" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2189f101-13f3-56fd-94db-1f1071ee6a01" :question/math-academy-id "q-83868" :question/problem "The growth rate of the crab population $P(t)$ in a certain river is jointly proportional to the crab population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the river's carrying capacity and the crab population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,02$ and the carrying capacity is $10\\,000,$ then find an expression for the crab population after $t$ years, given that there were $1\\,000$ crab in the river initially." :question/worked-solution "Since the crab population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the crab population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the crab population, the crab population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,02,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=10\\,000.$ Therefore, the crab population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,02P(10\\,000-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{10\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(10\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,02t)}}=\\frac{10\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.2t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=1\\,000$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.2\\cdot 0}} & =1\\,000 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{10\\,000}{1\\,000} \\\\ C & =10-1 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t)=\\frac{10\\,000}{1+9{e}^{-0.2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e71ba74-f952-56ef-885c-a32c4a4aec20" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Logistic Growth Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5667cf1-af77-58a2-b72c-d6f61340dfa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+5{e}^{-0.5t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327375} {:answer/id #uuid "5b2bb338-8323-5a37-85df-4d3475e72dfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+7{e}^{-0.5t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327376} {:answer/id #uuid "e93486dd-58e8-5462-8e07-7e15bf46c002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+4{e}^{-0.1t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327377} {:answer/id #uuid "27d2d6b5-cb2e-5d17-8073-8869cb3e29f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+5{e}^{-0.2t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327378} {:answer/id #uuid "e227422d-dcc6-5b54-9f4b-d9f22cfdb811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+4{e}^{-2t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b2bb338-8323-5a37-85df-4d3475e72dfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+7{e}^{-0.5t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327376} :answer-field/id #uuid "bde01042-6422-5410-8ce9-f190084bf700" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "deb24d9c-7f82-577b-9801-7cd591df4750" :question/math-academy-id "q-83875" :question/problem "The growth rate of the mouse population $P(t)$ in a certain field is jointly proportional to the mouse population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the field's carrying capacity and the mouse population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,062\\,5$ and the carrying capacity is $8\\,000,$ then find an expression for the mouse population after $t$ years, given that there were $1\\,000$ mice in the field initially." :question/worked-solution "Since the mouse population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the mouse population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the mouse population, the mouse population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,062\\,5,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=8\\,000.$ Therefore, the mouse population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,062\\,5P(8\\,000-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(8\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,062\\,5)t}}=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.5t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=1\\,000$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.5\\cdot 0}} & =1\\,000 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{8\\,000}{1\\,000} \\\\ C & =8-1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+7{e}^{-0.5t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18af80e5-cd0f-5a1d-b299-2956fd886c5d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Particular Solution of a Logistic Growth Initial Value Problem to Make Predictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2270adc-ac34-5a91-96c4-38b5616bdc6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327382} {:answer/id #uuid "1f4192d8-01d9-5fee-a36b-a00545f09a48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327383} {:answer/id #uuid "c3675c40-3e87-56e8-bdc6-7450f2fbacbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327384} {:answer/id #uuid "6b5d7d5e-b28c-5e87-884c-92e31f19694d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327385} {:answer/id #uuid "58565b70-0e3b-53a1-bcf3-dccaa82d446b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3675c40-3e87-56e8-bdc6-7450f2fbacbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327384} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccdf6183-a13b-5676-b67b-e64e469c9685" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc96676a-202b-5c2e-ae13-566e31d66cbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-83895" :question/problem "The population $P(t)$ of turtles on a particular island follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.05P-0.000\\,5{P}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. If there were $5$ turtles on the island initially, how many turtles inhabited the island after $11$ years? Round you answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe start by factoring the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.05P-0.000\\,5{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.000\\,5P(100-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,5,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=100.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{100}{1+C{e}^{-(100\\cdot 0.000\\,5)t}}=\\frac{100}{1+C{e}^{-0.05t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=5$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =5 \\\\ \\frac{100}{1+C{e}^{-0.05(0)}} & =5 \\\\ 1+C & =20 \\\\ C & =19\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{100}{1+19{e}^{-0.05t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=11$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(11) & =\\frac{100}{1+19{e}^{-0.05(11)}} \\\\  & ≈8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $11$ years, approximately $8$ turtles inhabited the island."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e71ba74-f952-56ef-885c-a32c4a4aec20" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Logistic Growth Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad7e000-9a43-586f-9040-0a3f8dc0336f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{3\\,000}{1-29{e}^{-900t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327389} {:answer/id #uuid "9381805b-ffb0-5b7f-b57f-b2faebadf8be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{4\\,500}{1-29{e}^{-900t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327390} {:answer/id #uuid "7fa3dde8-86c0-5e8b-b811-ddb7b69ce23a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{4\\,500}{1+150{e}^{-900t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327391} {:answer/id #uuid "17a6d571-d5ca-5a1b-99bc-db8a3512506c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{4\\,500}{1+29{e}^{-90t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327392} {:answer/id #uuid "77df335e-22ae-5204-8b78-e56937680d52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{4\\,500}{1+29{e}^{-900t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327393}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77df335e-22ae-5204-8b78-e56937680d52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{4\\,500}{1+29{e}^{-900t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327393} :answer-field/id #uuid "cde39490-7ba7-5a36-af6a-63005aac1168" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9caecca5-59a6-5277-9b1d-c52fd8f94958" :question/math-academy-id "q-85977" :question/problem "The rate of growth of the bear population $P(t)$ in a certain forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P(4\\,500-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years. Find an expression for the bear population after $t$ years, given that there were $150$ bears initially." :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.2,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=4\\,500.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{4\\,500}{1+C{e}^{-(4\\,500\\cdot 0.2)t}}=\\frac{4\\,500}{1+C{e}^{-900t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=150$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4\\,500}{1+C{e}^{-900\\cdot 0}} & =150 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{4\\,500}{150} \\\\ C & =30-1 \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{4\\,500}{1+29{e}^{-900t}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18af80e5-cd0f-5a1d-b299-2956fd886c5d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Particular Solution of a Logistic Growth Initial Value Problem to Make Predictions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dbad54b-2ee9-52bd-968c-964f1e4225e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8791" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327396} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a0d465-d7f0-5af3-8f7e-c9a9b802d1d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6159" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327397} {:answer/id #uuid "3da6e52a-4a5d-5800-a267-cbf6bd3848bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7787" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327398} {:answer/id #uuid "d75ce1b8-8d4b-5dc1-9861-791f906c80e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6897" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327399} {:answer/id #uuid "8d971a9d-fdf3-5cfe-a267-379ef0aaeeab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7798" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1a0d465-d7f0-5af3-8f7e-c9a9b802d1d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6159" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327397} :answer-field/id #uuid "e619e6f5-6109-555e-b729-2ee56268cfcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327395}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a9d08af3-2742-523b-8f61-df60045fe64c" :question/math-academy-id "q-85978" :question/problem "The growth rate of the deer population $P(t)$ in a certain forest is jointly proportional to the deer population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the forest's carrying capacity and the deer population. The constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,03$ and the carrying capacity is $9000.$ How many deers will there be in the forest after $8$ years given that there were $1800$ deers initially? Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "Since the deer population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the deer population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the deer population, the deer population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,03,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=9000.$ Therefore, the deer population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,03P(9000-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{9000}{1+C{e}^{-(9000\\cdot 0.000\\,03)t}}=\\frac{9000}{1+C{e}^{-0.27t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=1800$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =1800 \\\\ \\frac{9000}{1+C{e}^{-0.27(0)}} & =1800 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{9000}{1800} \\\\ C & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{9000}{1+4{e}^{-0.27\\cdot t}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $t=8$ into the expression for $P(t),$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(8) & =\\frac{9000}{1+4{e}^{-0.27\\cdot 8}} \\\\  & ≈6159.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
